  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nWarm and dry conditions will come to an end as a low pressure\nsystem currently tracking across the Midwest advances eastward\ninto the Ohio River Valley. Precipitation will initially remain\nall rain Thursday morning into after sunset as the low pivots \nacross western Pennsylvania and continues eastward. However, \nstronger cold air advection Thursday night into early Friday \nwill support the transition from rain to snow. \n\nThe highest snowfall accumulations are expected across the\nridges, particularly in Tucker County, where 1 to 3 inches of\nsnow will be possible. Orographic lift, combined with sufficient\nmoisture within the dendritic growth zone (DGZ), will support \nlight to moderate snowfall rates (0.1 to 0.4 inches per hour) \nacross parts of the ridges between 2am and 9am Friday. \n\nNarrow bands of lake-enhanced snow showers may develop Friday\ninto Saturday between Cleveland, potentially resulting in minor\naccumulations on grassy surfaces. However, the low-to-mid level\nflow off the Great Lakes is expected to be weak, limiting the\npotential for prolonged and intense lake-effect snow. At the\nmoment, Tucker County remains at the border for Advisory level\nsnow. A higher-end scenario, with localized snowfall totals of\n4-5 inches in eastern Tucker would require persistent, training\nsnow showers to develop. \n\nWith a building ridge over the Midwest and Great Lakes Sunday,\nthe probability of snow will gradually decrease throughout the\nday. Dry and quiet weather are expected Sunday night into \nWednesday.",
